// Oracle question: does a `[ … ]` block opening the line after a declaration
// belong to the declaration?
//
// K65 is newline-insensitive, so the question is a real one: `const BASE =
// 0x10` followed by a line that opens with `[` could be one item or two.  The
// original has no postfix to offer - `Const -> Ident = Number` ends where the
// number does, and `Var -> Ident [BracketNumber] [= Number]` takes at most one
// bracket and takes it before the `=` - so it reads two, and this golden is
// that answer.
//
// K816 read one.  Its own symbolic subscripts give the expression layer a
// postfix `[`, so `const BASE = 0x10` above `[ STEP = 3 ]` parsed as
// `BASE = 0x10[STEP = 3]` and reported `K816-E0001` - a generic parse error on
// a program the original compiles, which is the shape decision 0 exists to
// prevent.  Manifest row `newline-subscript`; closed in G10a by making that
// postfix bind to its base with no space between, exactly as `.field` already
// did.
//
// All three declaration shapes that end in a value are asked, because the
// reading was per-shape: an initialized `const`, an addressed `var`, and a
// `var` whose own bracket is already spent on a span and is addressed as
// well.

const BASE = 0x10
[ STEP = 3 ]

var slot = 0x80
[ COUNT = 4 ]

var span[4] = 0x90
[ MASK = 0x0F ]

data answers {
  [ BASE + STEP ]
  [ COUNT ]
  [ MASK ]
}

main {
  a = answers
  return
}
